{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pairing method for arithmetic sums pairing method for arithmetic sums","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md","fileName":"Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Sequences and Algebraic Patterns]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequences and Algebraic Patterns","Arithmetic Progression","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"pairing method for arithmetic sums pairing method for arithmetic sums","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md","fileName":"Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums","heading":"Pairing Method for Arithmetic Sums","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The pairing method for arithmetic sums combines terms equally far from opposite ends of an [[Arithmetic Progression]]. Each pair has the same total.\n\nFor (1+2+\\cdots+100), pairing (1) with (100), (2) with (99), and so on gives fifty pairs of (101), so the sum is (50\\cdot101=5050). Equivalently, write the sum forward and backward, add the two rows to obtain one hundred copies of (101), and divide by two. The method exposes structure that direct accumulation hides.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequences and Algebraic Patterns","Arithmetic Progression","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"